{"id":1037,"date":"2026-03-23T04:31:07","date_gmt":"2026-03-23T04:31:07","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=1037"},"modified":"2026-03-23T04:32:13","modified_gmt":"2026-03-23T04:32:13","slug":"%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e4%ba%92%e6%98%93%e6%80%a7%e4%b8%8e%e5%8a%a8%e6%80%81%e6%98%9f%e5%ba%a7%e6%b7%b7%e6%b7%86%e7%9a%84%e6%97%a0%e7%ba%bf%e9%80%9a%e4%bf%a1%e5%ae%89%e5%85%a8%e6%96%b9","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e5%9f%ba%e4%ba%8e%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e4%ba%92%e6%98%93%e6%80%a7%e4%b8%8e%e5%8a%a8%e6%80%81%e6%98%9f%e5%ba%a7%e6%b7%b7%e6%b7%86%e7%9a%84%e6%97%a0%e7%ba%bf%e9%80%9a%e4%bf%a1%e5%ae%89%e5%85%a8%e6%96%b9\/","title":{"rendered":"Wireless Communication Security Scheme Based on Channel Reciprocity and Dynamic Constellation Obfuscation"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Wireless Communication Security Scheme Based on Channel Reciprocity and Dynamic Constellation Obfuscation<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">HOU Y, SUN H, LI G, et al<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Internet of Things Journal<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/Securing_Wireless_Communications_via_Channel_Reciprocity_and_Dynamic_Constellation_Obfuscation.pdf\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/JIOT.2025.3581791\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809 noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>The one-time-pad secure transmission based on wireless channel reciprocity (CR-OTP) has attracted widespread attention due to its perfect secrecy in simultaneously protecting both data and modulation information. However, existing CR-OTP schemes face dual challenges of reliability and security in practical applications\u2014the assumptions of channel reciprocity and randomness are not always satisfied. To address these issues, this paper proposes a dynamic constellation confusion method that dynamically rotates the constellation diagram to confuse the plaintext. This analog encryption method is proven to be more robust than existing digital XOR encryption methods, achieving a lower symbol error rate by reducing the double quantization loss to a single one. The rotation pattern is jointly determined by the channel state information and the previous frame message, thereby ensuring the randomness of the rotation pattern in slowly varying channel environments. Only legitimate receivers that can correctly recover the previous frame message and observe similar CSI can decode the currently transmitted message. Theoretical analysis proves that the secrecy capacity of the DCO method is higher than that of existing optimal schemes. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed method excels in both secrecy capacity and SER: it achieves a 4.5 dB SNR gain at SER = 0.1; when the secrecy capacity is 0.1, the main channel SNR gain reaches 6.5 dB under an eavesdropping channel SNR of 20 dB.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes the DCO analog encryption scheme, reducing double quantization loss to a single one to enhance transmission reliability<\/strong>: Unlike traditional digital XOR encryption, which requires CSI quantization, DCO directly drives constellation rotation with analog CSI values, avoiding the secondary accumulation of quantization errors; theoretical derivation proves <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>Pr<\/mi><mo>\u2061<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi>Pr<\/mi><mo>\u2061<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\Pr_{DCO} &lt; \\Pr_{XOR}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, i.e., the SER of DCO is strictly lower than that of the XOR scheme, achieving a 4.5 dB SNR gain at SER = 0.1 under QPSK modulation.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Designs a time-varying rotation pattern generation method that jointly exploits channel randomness and message randomness to enhance security in slowly varying environments<\/strong>: The rotation angle is jointly determined by the current CSI and the previously transmitted message of the previous frame, with a chaotic sequence introduced to ensure the unpredictability of the rotation pattern; theoretical analysis proves that the average secrecy capacity of DCO is higher than that of the XOR scheme (<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo>&gt;<\/mo><msubsup><mi>C<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">C_s^{DCO} &gt; C_s^{XOR}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>), and at <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>\u03b3<\/mi><mo>\u02c9<\/mo><\/mover><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\bar{\\gamma}_m = 10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u03b3, the secrecy capacity gain reaches 0.1 bps, effectively resisting five typical threats including differential attacks, injection attacks, and channel replay attacks.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Comprehensively validates the security and reliability of the DCO scheme through theoretical analysis and simulation experiments<\/strong>: Comparisons with the XOR scheme across multiple dimensions, including secrecy capacity, outage probability, SER, and phase error robustness, consistently confirm the superiority of DCO; computational complexity is also analyzed, demonstrating the feasibility of DCO for hardware-constrained physical layer security implementations.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1038\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">The system adopts a classic three-party TDD-OFDM model (Alice, Bob, Eve), where Alice and Bob alternately transmit pilots to perform channel sampling and obtain highly correlated channel estimates <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo>^<\/mo><\/mover><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\hat{h}_A(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>h<\/mi><mo>^<\/mo><\/mover><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\hat{h}_B(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3002<strong>DCO encryption process<\/strong>is divided into two core modules:<strong>Constellation rotation pattern generation module<\/strong>jointly processes the phase component of the current CSI and the previous frame message through a chaotic map (Logistic map) to generate a time-varying rotation angle sequence <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>\u03b8<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\theta(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, whose randomness originates from both channel randomness and message randomness;<strong>In the constellation confusion and clarification module<\/strong>, Alice applies rotational encryption to the modulated symbol <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">x(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> as <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>\u22c5<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>\u03b8<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">x_c(k) = x(k) \\cdot e^{j\\theta(k)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>before transmission, and Bob reconstructs the rotation angle using his own CSI and performs inverse rotation <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x<\/mi><mo>^<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>\u22c5<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mo>\u2212<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>\u03b8<\/mi><mo>^<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\hat{x}(k) = x_c(k) \\cdot e^{-j\\hat{\\theta}(k)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> for decryption. Since Eve cannot obtain CSI related to Bob and is unaware of the previous frame message, the received constellation points are uniformly distributed across the entire constellation space, preventing the recovery of valid information.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-dda34f26 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-07.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-07.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-07.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-07.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1040\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-07\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.12<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-28-00.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-28-00.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-28-00.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-28-00.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1039\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_12-28-00\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.14<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>The DCO scheme achieves a 4.5 dB SNR gain over the XOR scheme at SER = 0.1, and a 6.5 dB main channel SNR gain when the secrecy capacity outage probability is 0.1 (<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>\u03b3<\/mi><mo>\u02c9<\/mo><\/mover><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\bar{\\gamma}_w = 20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>Fig. 14<\/strong> As shown, the SER of all schemes decreases with increasing SNR, but the DCO scheme consistently outperforms the XOR scheme, leading by 4.5 dB at SER = 0.1 under QPSK modulation, validating the reliability advantage of analog encryption in avoiding double quantization loss.<strong>Fig. 12<\/strong> The outage probability curves in <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">R_s = 0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3001<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>\u03b3<\/mi><mo>\u02c9<\/mo><\/mover><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\bar{\\gamma}_w = 20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> Under dB conditions, the outage probability curve of the DCO scheme shifts right by approximately 6.5 dB compared to the XOR scheme, demonstrating its stronger secrecy capacity. Furthermore,<strong>Fig. 13<\/strong> shows that the legitimate receiver's SER approaches 0, while the initial values differ by only <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mo>\u2212<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">10^{-15}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>10\u221215, the illegitimate receiver's SER reaches as high as 0.7, intuitively verifying the high sensitivity of the chaotic key to initial values.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-080de2b0 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-16.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-16.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-16.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-16.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1041\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-23_12-29-16\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><figcaption class=\"uagb-image-caption\">Fig.13<\/figcaption><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{hou2025dco,\n  author    = {Yujie Hou and Haixi Sun and Guyue Li and Shuping Dang and Aiqun Hu},\n  title     = {Securing Wireless Communications via Channel Reciprocity and Dynamic Constellation Obfuscation},\n  journal   = {IEEE Internet of Things Journal},\n  volume    = {12},\n  number    = {18},\n  pages     = {37035--37050},\n  month     = sep,\n  year      = {2025},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/JIOT.2025.3581791}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Secret key generation based on wireless channel reciprocity for one-time pad secure transmission (CR-OTP) has attracted widespread attention due to its perfect secrecy in protecting both data and modulation information. However, existing CR-OTP schemes face dual challenges of reliability and security in practical applications\u2014the assumptions of channel reciprocity and randomness are not always satisfied. To address these issues, this paper proposes a dynamic constellation obfuscation (DCO) method, which dynamically rotates the constellation diagram to obfuscate the plaintext. This analog encryption method is proven to be more robust than existing digital XOR encryption methods, achieving a lower symbol error rate (SER) by reducing the double quantization loss to a single one. The rotation pattern is jointly determined by channel state information (CSI) and the previous frame message, thereby ensuring the randomness of the rotation pattern in slowly varying channel environments. Only legitimate receivers that can correctly recover the previous frame message and observe similar CSI can decode the current transmitted message. Theoretical analysis proves that the secrecy capacity of the DCO method is higher than that of existing optimal schemes. Simulation results demonstrate the superiority of the proposed method in both secrecy capacity and SER: it achieves a 4.5 dB SNR gain at an SER of 0.1; when the secrecy capacity is 0.1, the main channel SNR gain reaches 6.5 dB under an eavesdropper channel SNR of 20 dB.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1038,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1037","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05.png",710,425,false],"thumbnail":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-23_12-27-05-150x150.png",150,150,true],"medium":["https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/w